# Slide Announcer pre-provisioning config — see docs/BUILDING.md's
# "Pre-provisioning network + identity" for the full walkthrough.
#
# NOTE: comments in this file do NOT survive the device's first boot.
# provisioning/firstboot.py parses this file with a plain YAML load/dump
# round-trip (no comment-preserving parser) the first time it writes
# device_uuid/device_uuid_check back here, which happens on every
# device's very first boot (and again after any factory reset). Values
# and keys you set below survive that rewrite; these comments won't —
# edit this file before first power-on if you want the documentation to
# be useful to whoever mounts the card next.
#
# WiFi/network settings do NOT go in this file — edit network-config
# (also on this boot partition) instead. This file only ever covers
# identity and initial-setup hints.

# Default UI language hint for initial setup only — "en" or "es". Used
# once, to pick a sensible starting language for the first setup screen
# before anyone has configured the device. Not a permanent setting: it
# can be changed at any time afterward from the device's own Settings
# menu, and doing so has no effect on this file.
default_language: en

# Set to "locked" to disable Ctrl+Alt+F# virtual-console switching on this
# device (system/scripts/vtlock-apply.py, run once per boot before the
# kiosk starts). Any other value, or omitting the key entirely, leaves VT
# switching alone — the kernel default. Unlike default_language above, this
# one IS read on every boot, not just the first: it's not a one-time
# provisioning hint, just a config value that happens to live in this file.
# Edit it on the boot partition and reboot (or power-cycle) to change it.
# vt_lockswitch: locked

# device_uuid / device_uuid_check: leave these out. firstboot.py
# generates a fresh, consistent pair on the device's first boot — see
# provisioning/README.md and SLIDE_ANNOUNCER.md's "Device identity &
# anti-clone protection". Hand-editing device_uuid without the matching
# secret (which never leaves /data) just triggers a fresh identity
# regeneration on the next boot rather than doing anything useful.
